;;; A `std.macro` macro the program does not import, written as a fact, is stored as a fact of its
;;; name —
;;; regression `macro-call-stdlib-unimported`.
;;;
;;; ── What this program is ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; Two guests and the fact `(unknown (paid Bob))`, with no import. No candidate.
;;;
;;; ── The answer, by hand ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; A rule whose `:match` writes `(unknown …)` without importing `std.macro` is refused, and so
;;; is such a goal: unexpanded, the call would never match. A fact
;;; of that name is the same call, and no premise of any program can read it. Refused at load,
;;; at the fact, one line, on every command.
;;;
;;; ── What the engine answered ────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; At `91b12be8`, release: the program loads; `(unknown (paid Bob))` is stored. R is {∅}, exit
;;; 0 on every command.
;;; The engine refuses it at load.
;;;

(relation paid Guest :why "{?3} has paid")
(is-a Ann Guest) (is-a Bob Guest)
(unknown (paid Bob))
(solve :hypothesis-relations ())
(query :goal (paid ?g))
